<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">United States of Colombia</h1><p class="st-meta">1863 CE – 1886 CE · capital Bogotá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A radically federal Colombian republic of nine sovereign states, created by the Liberals in 1863 and ended by the centralist constitution of 1886.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After winning the civil war of 1860 to 1862 against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/granadine-confederation">Granadine Confederation</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-cipriano-de-mosquera">Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera</a> and the Liberals met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rionegro">Rionegro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioquia-department">Antioquia</a> and adopted a new constitution in 1863, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Colombia</a>, made up of nine sovereign states including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rionegro">Rionegro</a> constitution gave the states their own armies and laws, limited the president to a two-year term and guaranteed broad freedoms, including freedom of the press and of trade in arms. The Radical Liberals separated church and state and promoted secular public schools. The weak central government struggled with frequent regional civil wars, notably the church-backed war of 1876 and 1877.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-nunez">Rafael Núñez</a> led a movement called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regeneration-colombia">Regeneration</a>. After the civil war of 1885, the constitution of 1886 restored a centralised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia</a> with strong ties to the Catholic Church.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-of-colombia.jpg" alt="United States of Colombia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colombia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1863 CE – 1886 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bogotá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera, Manuel Murillo Toro, Rafael Núñez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estados Unidos de Colombia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=united-states-of-colombia" aria-label="United States of Colombia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2625 2005 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United States of Colombia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2945 2261L2945 2261M2945 2261L2945 2261"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2945 2261h0M2945 2261h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2945 2261h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2945 2261h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bogotá. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=united-states-of-colombia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
